WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.449
Threat modeling examines what a system
must protect and how its design could permit

2
00:00:04.529 --> 00:00:05.488
unwanted outcomes.

3
00:00:05.568 --> 00:00:08.615
Architecture documents, data flows,
identity relationships,

4
00:00:08.695 --> 00:00:10.726
and control descriptions provide inputs.

5
00:00:10.806 --> 00:00:14.247
AI can organize assets and trust
boundaries, suggest threat scenarios,

6
00:00:14.327 --> 00:00:16.697
and compare controls with proposed attack
paths.

7
00:00:16.777 --> 00:00:20.387
A useful result identifies assumptions and
control gaps for design review,

8
00:00:20.467 --> 00:00:22.781
rather than simply producing a long threat
list.

9
00:00:22.861 --> 00:00:25.343
Begin with a defined system and a concrete
workflow,

10
00:00:25.423 --> 00:00:29.372
such as a customer uploading a document
that a background service later processes.

11
00:00:29.452 --> 00:00:33.345
Give the model a reviewed description of
components, data movement, identities,

12
00:00:33.425 --> 00:00:34.553
and existing controls.

13
00:00:34.633 --> 00:00:38.582
Ask it to identify where data or authority
crosses a trust boundary and which assets

14
00:00:38.662 --> 00:00:39.565
could be affected.

15
00:00:39.645 --> 00:00:43.482
Record missing information as questions
instead of allowing the model to invent

16
00:00:43.562 --> 00:00:45.028
architecture or protections.

17
00:00:45.108 --> 00:00:48.663
For each candidate threat, trace the
actor, entry point, required access,

18
00:00:48.743 --> 00:00:51.789
and sequence of actions that could produce
an unwanted outcome.

19
00:00:51.869 --> 00:00:52.998
In the upload workflow,

20
00:00:53.078 --> 00:00:56.632
ask whether one customer's request could
cause the service to read another

21
00:00:56.712 --> 00:00:57.784
customer's document.

22
00:00:57.864 --> 00:01:00.064
Check where object permissions are
enforced,

23
00:01:00.144 --> 00:01:02.288
which identity the background service
uses,

24
00:01:02.368 --> 00:01:05.302
and whether an alternative path bypasses
the expected check.

25
00:01:05.382 --> 00:01:08.823
A generated scenario matters only when its
preconditions fit the actual

26
00:01:08.903 --> 00:01:10.483
implementation and deployment.

27
00:01:10.563 --> 00:01:13.158
AI can compare proposed mitigations with
those paths,

28
00:01:13.238 --> 00:01:14.648
draft abuse cases for review,

29
00:01:14.728 --> 00:01:17.549
and turn unresolved assumptions into
verification tasks.

30
00:01:17.629 --> 00:01:20.958
Connect each mitigation to the relevant
asset and failure condition.

31
00:01:21.038 --> 00:01:25.043
A recommendation to add authorization
checks becomes useful when it identifies the

32
00:01:25.123 --> 00:01:27.606
operation, the identity-to-object
relationship,

33
00:01:27.686 --> 00:01:29.942
and a test that would demonstrate
enforcement.

34
00:01:30.022 --> 00:01:33.069
Reviewers then confirm feasibility and
assign follow-up work.

35
00:01:33.149 --> 00:01:36.816
The deliverable is a traceable threat
model: a reviewed system description,

36
00:01:36.896 --> 00:01:40.958
relevant scenarios, supporting evidence,
control gaps, and unresolved questions.

37
00:01:41.038 --> 00:01:44.197
Revisit it when architecture, permissions,
or data flows change.

38
00:01:44.277 --> 00:01:47.324
AI can broaden the discussion and reduce
documentation effort;

39
00:01:47.404 --> 00:01:51.297
security and engineering specialists
validate the paths and decide which design

40
00:01:51.377 --> 00:01:52.900
changes and tests are required.
